     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>17-Year-Old Transforms Bengaluru School Storage Room into 2,000-Book Library</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Community</span> <span>#Education</span> <span>#Youth Initiative</span> <span>#Bengaluru</span></div>
       <p>Asavari Agrawal, a 17-year-old from Bengaluru, has, for nearly five years, dedicated her weekends to children at government schools and residential homes, fostering deep connections. At Mullur Colony Government School, children enthusiastically greet her, often calling her &quot;Didi&quot; or &quot;Amma,&quot; a testament to her consistent presence. Asavari founded &quot;Shape the Future&quot; at age 13, an initiative now involving around 50 student volunteers who reach approximately 250 children through various activities, including sports sessions, health camps, and learning programs.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/17-year-old-transforms-bengaluru-school-storage-room-into-20.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The most significant recent accomplishment is the transformation of an unused storage room at Mullur Colony Government School into a vibrant library boasting over 2,000 books. The idea originated from conversations where children expressed a desire for reading material beyond their textbooks. Over nearly two years, Asavari and five student volunteers diligently collected donated books from various schools and communities. The physical transformation of the room involved extensive work, including redoing the flooring, repainting walls, cleaning, and installing shelves and furniture to house the extensive collection. Headmistress Victoria lauded the effort, stating, &quot;Before this we had nothing. Only textbooks. No library, nothing like that. She collected more than two thousand books and arranged the room also with shelves and light. For our school this is a very big thing.&quot;</p><p>Despite balancing Class 12 studies, college applications, national-level table tennis, Bharatanatyam, and Grade 8 piano, Asavari remains committed to her volunteering work. She emphasizes that &quot;showing up is what matters most&quot; to her NGO, prioritizing emotional and physical presence for the children over numbers or one-off events.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a well-structured, accurate summary that captures the essence of Asavari's initiative and the library project.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/changemakers/asavari-agrawal-shape-the-future-bengaluru-government-school-children-library-teen-12807047" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/bengaluru/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bengaluru</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
