     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>California Governor Newsom Endorses Global Fossil Fuel Phaseout at Cop31</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#Fossil Fuels</span> <span>#Global Policy</span> <span>#California</span></div>
       <p>California Governor Gavin Newsom, who leads the world’s fourth biggest economy, has explicitly endorsed a global phaseout of fossil fuels, the primary drivers of climate breakdown. Speaking at the Cop31 UN climate summit on Thursday in Antalya, Turkey, Newsom emerged as the most prominent US politician present, contrasting with the Trump administration's ongoing boycott of the process. He told the Guardian that California is &quot;100%&quot; behind the efforts of the Santa Marta coalition, a group of over 50 countries, including many of the world's largest economies, which aims to transition away from oil, gas, and coal towards solar, wind, batteries, and other non-carbon energy sources.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/california-governor-newsom-endorses-global-fossil-fuel-phase.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Newsom, as the head of America’s most populous and economically powerful state, questioned the delay in adopting clean energy, stating, &quot;When you have a product that is obviously superior, which clean energy is for the climate and the economy, why wouldn’t you make that shift as quickly as possible?&quot; His statement raises the possibility that the US, the world’s biggest economy and largest historical emitter of planet-warming gases, could join the Santa Marta coalition if Newsom, widely expected to run for president in 2028, wins the presidency two years from now. The Santa Marta coalition formed after more than 80 countries' efforts to develop a global &quot;roadmap&quot; to phase out fossil fuels were blocked at last year’s Cop30 by a handful of petrostates, led by Saudi Arabia, which relied on the disputed notion that decisions at UN climate summits require consensus. Undaunted, countries favoring a roadmap met in April in Santa Marta, Colombia, to begin designing a phase out on their own.</p><p>Newsom expressed his approval for the coalition's challenge, remarking, &quot;Love it,&quot; regarding the &quot;coalition of the willing&quot; that is pushing back against states intent on maintaining fossil fuel use. This critical mass of countries, which currently includes Germany, France, Brazil, and The Netherlands, with the UK also expected to join later in Cop31, is proceeding with roadmaps to transition away from fossil fuels, hoping their example and economic power will lead others to follow suit. Mohamed Nasheed, the former president of The Maldives, underscored this sentiment, stating, &quot;You don’t ask drug dealers to vote for Prohibition, and you won’t get petro-states to agree to phase out fossil fuels. Countries wishing to phase out fossil fuels should just go ahead and do it.&quot; Germany, the world’s third biggest economy, has committed to phasing out fossil fuels by 2045, while France, the sixth biggest, and The Netherlands, the 18th biggest, have already released their own phase-out roadmaps.</p><p>The coalition’s collective economic might, representing the biggest economic superpower on Earth among the 80-plus countries that sought a phase-out roadmap at Cop30, makes its vision more than aspirational. Mohamed Adow, director of Power Shift Africa, noted that successful implementation of a credible transition &quot;would send an unmistakable message that the age of oil, gas and coal is ending, and the smart money is shifting,&quot; signaling increased risk for continued fossil fuel investments. California's own clean energy track record aligns with a potential Cop31 commitment to obtain 35% of the world’s energy from electricity by 2035; the state's economy ran on 100% clean electricity nine out of every ten days during much of 2026, with batteries proving to be &quot;a game changer.&quot; Recent political shifts, including the re-election of President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva of Brazil in October and the rebuke of Trump and his Republican Congress in the US midterm elections in early November, have also raised hopes for a new era in global climate politics, bringing scores of climate champions to power at state and local levels, such as Democrat Gina Hinojosa, the new governor in Texas, who shunned fossil fuel industry contributions.</p><p>What to watch: Continued expansion of the Santa Marta coalition and the practical implementation of national phase-out roadmaps.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively captures the political context, the specific coalition mentioned, and the scope of the COP31 developments.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/13/california-gavin-newsom-endorses-fossil-fuel-phaseout" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
