     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Centre Mandates EVs for Official Vehicles, Raises Purchase Limit</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Government Policy</span> <span>#Electric Vehicles</span> <span>#Finance</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>The Central government has designated electric vehicles (EVs) as the default choice for official cars utilized by central government ministries and departments. This directive, outlined in an office memorandum dated November 10, 2026, from the Department of Expenditure under the Ministry of Finance, revises the rules governing the procurement and hiring of staff cars. It sets a higher Net Dealer Price (NDP) ceiling of ₹20 lakh for electric vehicles, compared to ₹10 lakh for conventional internal combustion engine (ICE) vehicles, which include petrol, diesel, and hybrid models. This move aims to accelerate EV adoption across government fleets and reduce reliance on fossil fuels.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/centre-mandates-evs-for-official-vehicles-raises-purchase-li.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Under the revised guidelines, government departments are now expected to select EVs when replacing existing petrol or diesel staff cars. Any department seeking to purchase conventional vehicles must provide a justified proposal demonstrating why an EV is impractical for their specific requirements, and then secure approval through a specified process. This proposal necessitates approval from the secretary of the concerned administrative ministry or department and concurrence from its financial adviser. The decision is part of a broader governmental strategy to promote electric mobility, reduce fossil fuel dependence, and account for current vehicle prices and lifecycle costs.</p><p>The higher NDP ceiling for electric vehicles provides ministries and departments greater flexibility in considering various EV models within their procurement budgets. Importantly, these revised purchase ceilings are administrative procurement guidelines and do not translate into a personal car purchase allowance or any direct monetary benefit for individual government employees. The rules apply exclusively to staff cars procured or hired by government entities for official use, and do not introduce a new car allowance, vehicle loan facility, or personal EV purchase subsidy. This development is also distinct from the ongoing work of the 8th Central Pay Commission concerning revisions to salaries, allowances, and pensions.</p><p>Furthermore, the Finance Ministry has retained existing instructions mandating the hiring of electric vehicles by ministries and departments. Similar to purchases, any consideration to hire petrol, diesel, or hybrid vehicles will also require a justified proposal and relaxation approval from the Department of Expenditure, with the necessary secretarial and financial adviser concurrences. The revised memorandum, which came into immediate effect from November 10, 2026, does not alter other existing conditions governing the entitlement, procurement, and hiring of staff cars, which continue to be governed by the General Financial Rules (GFR), 2017. For ordinary government employees, the immediate impact of this decision is primarily a change in the type of official vehicles their departments are expected to acquire, rather than a personal financial gain.</p><p>What to watch: The pace of EV adoption within government fleets and potential future expansions of this policy.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a thorough and accurate summary of the procurement policy changes, clarifying the distinction between official and personal use.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/government-employees-centre-sets-20-lakh-limit-for-electric-cars-makes-evs-default-choice-for-official-vehicles-11794569067726.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
