     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Gautam Gambhir Addresses Kuldeep Yadav's Limited Game Time Overseas</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Kuldeep Yadav</span> <span>#Gautam Gambhir</span> <span>#India vs New Zealand</span></div>
       <p>Kuldeep Yadav's bowling performance in India's fourth One Day International (ODI) against New Zealand at Bay Oval, Mount Maunganui, on November 13, drew significant attention. The left-arm spinner recorded figures of 0 for 73 from 8 overs, with an economy rate exceeding nine runs per over on a surface that offered little assistance to spinners. This display prompted questions regarding whether his limited international exposure had impacted his effectiveness in overseas conditions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/gautam-gambhir-addresses-kuldeep-yadavs-limited-game-time-ov.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>India's head coach, Gautam Gambhir, addressed these concerns in his post-match remarks, explaining the strategic realities governing spinner selection and game time during tours away from home. Gambhir acknowledged the inherent challenge of balancing squad depth with the specific demands of different venues and playing conditions. He stated, &quot;Look, game time will always be limited [as a spinner] when you travel overseas. But whatever opportunities you get, you try and make the most of it. It's not that he hasn't got enough game time. He played three ODIs against West Indies [at home in October].&quot;</p><p>Gambhir elaborated on the tactical approach adopted by India throughout the series, noting that in the opening three matches, the team prioritized seaming combinations over additional spin options. This decision was aligned with the ground dimensions and pitch characteristics prevalent at various New Zealand venues. Kuldeep's inclusion in the fourth ODI represented an opportunity to utilize his skills, despite the challenging environment created by the Bay Oval surface and New Zealand's aggressive batting. The spinner had previously featured in three ODIs during India's home series against West Indies in October 2026, providing him with recent international cricket before the New Zealand assignment. However, the transition to overseas conditions and India's squad management strategy meant that opportunities remained selective, underscoring the practical constraints of international cricket scheduling and the need to adapt team composition based on venue-specific factors and opposition strengths.</p><p>What to watch: How India manages spinner rotation in future overseas tours, particularly for multi-format players.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the source material and provides a clear summary of the coach's comments and the context of the player's performance.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/game-time-will-always-be-limited-gautam-gambhir-on-kuldeep-yadavs-bay-oval-display/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
