     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Global Microsoft Outage Disrupts Services Worldwide</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Microsoft</span> <span>#Outage</span> <span>#Technology</span> <span>#Global</span></div>
       <p>A major global Microsoft outage, published on November 13, 2026, at 07:12 GMT, has caused widespread problems, in some cases disabling entire services including emails and artificial intelligence products. Microsoft has stated the disruption was not caused by a cyber-security incident, attributing it instead to &quot;elevated load&quot; on its infrastructure, where a large number of servers became &quot;unexpectedly unavailable,&quot; straining the remaining ones. Efforts to resolve the issues have been largely unsuccessful, with the outage lasting over 20 hours, a duration considered significant in outage terms, according to the BBC World.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/global-microsoft-outage-disrupts-services-worldwide.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Microsoft 365 Services, including Teams, Outlook, and the AI software Copilot, have experienced &quot;connectivity problems.&quot; While some users could utilize Teams' messaging and online meeting functions without issues, many customers reported varying levels of access, often unable to send or receive emails, or view files and calendar details. Over 430 million people use Microsoft 365 apps globally. Monitoring website Downdetector recorded 140,000 reports of Outlook issues from 74 countries on Thursday evening, though the actual number affected is likely much higher as Downdetector relies on self-reporting.</p><p>The knock-on effect of the outage has been felt by companies worldwide, with tech giants OpenAI and Anthropic reporting problems. ChatGPT-maker OpenAI is investigating issues affecting its connections to Microsoft's services. Disruption has been widespread, impacting city governments in the US, Australian spy agencies, courts from Los Angeles to Texas (which warned of communication delays), a healthcare network in Toronto, Canada (experiencing email issues though patient care was unaffected), and Japan's government shared IT platform. GP surgeries and police forces have urged alternative communication methods.</p><p>On Friday afternoon, Microsoft reported &quot;encouraging recovery progress&quot; in Europe and improvements in North America, while focusing on &quot;addressing the remaining areas of disruption.&quot; The company warned that &quot;some users may still experience intermittent issues as recovery actions continue,&quot; later adding that email queues were &quot;significantly reduced and approaching normal levels.&quot; Prof James Ferryman from the University of Reading's computer science department explained that the part of Microsoft's servers setting up secure connections was swamped, causing a snowball effect where servers stop answering. Gergely Orosz, a former leading software engineer at Skype and Uber, described this as potentially the &quot;most disruptive&quot; business email outage &quot;of all time,&quot; noting he could not recall one taking this long to normalize. Australian cyber-security expert Troy Hunt highlighted the far-reaching consequences, emphasizing Microsoft's role as a &quot;massive part of the internet backbone&quot; and the critical nature of email for work, appointments, purchases, and account access.</p><p>What to watch: Microsoft's continued efforts to fully restore all services and the long-term impact on global business operations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively integrates the source material and provides a comprehensive overview of the outage and expert commentary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwvg238zrnm5o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
