     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Home Wood-Burning Linked to Significant Child Pollution Exposure</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Pollution</span> <span>#Wood-burning</span> <span>#PAH</span></div>
       <p>New research conducted across six European towns and cities suggests that children’s exposure to home wood-burning could be as significant as their exposure to traffic exhaust. The study focused on polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAH), a family of pollutants released when fuels are burned, measuring air quality in buildings where children spend much of their time, including schools, sports centres, and homes.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/home-wood-burning-linked-to-significant-child-pollution-expo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Dr. Barend van Drooge, senior author from Barcelona’s Institute for Environmental Assessment and Water Research (IEAWR), highlighted the health implications. He stated that some PAH compounds are linked to cancers following prolonged exposures, a fact recognized by the International Agency for Cancer Research, with existing evidence also suggesting these pollutants can delay cognitive development in children. Maria Antonia Aretaki, first author of the study, emphasized the importance of understanding pollutants in children's surroundings due to their vulnerability.</p><p>Measurements were taken in and around Athens, Barcelona, Colchester, Copenhagen, Helsinki, and Lisbon. The research found that urban areas with widespread wood-burning, specifically Colchester and Athens, exhibited the highest PAH levels. In contrast, Barcelona and Lisbon, where wood-burning is not commonly practiced, recorded the lowest concentrations. Notably, PAH exposure in Colchester and Athens exceeded World Health Organization guidelines.</p><p>Analyzing specific locations, sports centres consistently showed the lowest pollutant concentrations, followed by schools, although some schools, particularly in Lisbon, were impacted by nearby busy roads. Homes situated in areas with prevalent wood-burning often had some of the greatest PAH levels, with many featuring open fireplaces and stoves. The study also observed a clear seasonal pattern, with winter PAH concentrations significantly greater than those in summer. In Colchester homes during winter, high-molecular-weight PAHs were over ten times higher than in summer, while low-molecular-weight PAHs were approximately three times greater.</p><p>Dr. van Drooge expressed surprise at the findings, noting, &quot;Wood-burning is known to be an important emitter of PAH, but it was surprising that it has become such an important contributor to the overall presence of PAHs in urban areas.&quot; Despite these elevated levels, the study confirmed that none of the cities exceeded unacceptable limits for lifetime cancer risk.</p><p>The implications extend beyond the surveyed cities. Within the UK, similar impacts are likely not unique to Colchester, which ranked 120th out of 333 local councils for wood burner density; cities like Worthing, Norwich, and Reading had around four times more. A separate wintertime study in Anglesey found children in homes with wood burners exposed to over three times more particle pollution than those in non-wood-burning homes, with homes generally having more air pollution than schools. In Greece, wood-burning surged during the 2008 financial crisis due to soaring heating oil costs, contributing to increased particle pollution-related deaths in Athens, especially among those over 70. Some Greek towns experience PAH levels 100 times greater in winter compared to summer due to wood-burning.</p><p>What to watch: Policy responses to home wood-burning in urban areas.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent coverage of the study findings and context provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/13/wood-burning-stoves-pollution-traffic-exhaust-children" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
