     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>IIT Bhubaneswar Partners Coratia Technologies for Advanced Underwater Robotics</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#IIT Bhubaneswar</span> <span>#Coratia Technologies</span> <span>#Underwater Robotics</span> <span>#Defence</span></div>
       <p>The Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Bhubaneswar has formally partnered with Odisha-based Coratia Technologies Private Limited to undertake collaborative research aimed at developing advanced underwater communication systems and robotic platforms. This alliance, sealed with an agreement signed on Friday, targets potential applications crucial for defence, maritime security, infrastructure monitoring, and ocean exploration. The collaboration seeks to develop indigenous systems, bridging the gap between laboratory research and industry-ready solutions in a technology domain vital for India's strategic and maritime requirements.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/iit-bhubaneswar-partners-coratia-technologies-for-advanced-u.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>IIT Bhubaneswar brings expertise in underwater acoustic communication and signal processing, while Coratia Technologies is recognized for designing and developing unmanned underwater vehicles (UUVs). A primary focus of the partnership will be the creation of high-data-rate and robust underwater communication systems, which currently face significant technological challenges due to the inefficient travel of radio-frequency signals through water. Underwater communication largely relies on acoustic technologies, where factors such as signal attenuation, ambient noise, multipath propagation, and changing underwater conditions can impact data transmission reliability and speed. The collaboration aims to address these issues by integrating advancements in acoustic communication and signal processing with the practical demands of underwater robotic operations.</p><p>Beyond research, the two organizations plan to integrate advanced communication modules directly into underwater vehicles, enabling more reliable data exchange, monitoring, and control during critical underwater missions. The partnership also envisages joint technology development, prototyping, testing, and field validation to accelerate the transition of indigenous technologies into deployable systems. This will strengthen the capabilities of underwater vehicles for tasks like inspection, surveillance, and data collection in environments where human intervention is difficult, expensive, or hazardous. Potential applications extend to the inspection and monitoring of critical underwater infrastructure, including dams, bridges, ports, pipelines, and offshore assets, alongside defence, security, marine exploration, disaster management, and scientific research.</p><p>Debendra Pradhan, co-founder and CEO of Coratia Technologies, underscored the necessity of close collaboration between industry and academia to advance India's underwater technology capabilities. He noted that the partnership combines complementary expertise, facilitating the transition of research beyond the laboratory into practical applications and contributing to India's burgeoning underwater technology ecosystem. The alliance will also explore joint participation in research programs sponsored by the government, defence establishments, and industry, opening avenues for technology development in areas of national strategic importance. Coratia Technologies already holds a tripartite technology licensing agreement with the National Institute of Ocean Technology (NIOT) and the National Research Development Corporation (NRDC) for the autonomous underwater profiling drifter (AUPD, an indigenous ocean-monitoring technology capable of measuring temperature, salinity, and depth up to 2,000 meters. P R Sahu, a professor at IIT Bhubaneswar, added that the collaboration would align academic research more closely with industry requirements in this emerging and strategically vital domain, encouraging innovation and providing real-world challenges for students and researchers.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a comprehensive summary of the partnership, correctly identifying the organizations, objectives, and the specific technological focus. It uses all source material effectively.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/iit-bhubaneswar-coratia-technologies-tie-up-for-underwater-robotics-126111301902_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
