     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Records Significant Decline in TB Mortality, Treatment Success Rises</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Health</span> <span>#TB Elimination</span></div>
       <p>India has achieved a significant 38 percent reduction in TB mortality since 2015, as reported by the Union Health Ministry. This decline saw the estimated TB mortality rate decrease from 28 per lakh population in 2015 to 18 per lakh in 2025. According to figures released by the World Health Organization's (WHO) Global Tuberculosis Report 2026, India successfully reached the milestone of a 35 percent mortality reduction in 2025.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/india-records-significant-decline-in-tb-mortality-treatment-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Alongside this progress, India also recorded a 94 percent treatment success rate among patients with drug-susceptible TB. The Union Health Ministry attributed these advancements to intensified interventions implemented under the National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP). Key strategies included proactive case finding, the integration of AI-enabled X-rays, rapid diagnostics, and prompt treatment initiation.</p><p>Further efforts under the NTEP involved differentiated TB care and comprehensive nutritional support, notably through the Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana and the Ni-kshay Mitra initiative. The TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan played a crucial role, bringing over 25 crore people under intensified screening activities between December 7, 2024, and November 8, 2026.</p><p>This extensive campaign facilitated over 11 crore chest X-rays and more than three crore nucleic acid amplification tests (NAAT). During the specified period, 48.64 lakh new TB cases were identified, which included 14.97 lakh asymptomatic cases. The Ministry also highlighted the strategic deployment of portable AI-enabled digital X-rays and the expansion of rapid molecular diagnostic technologies, such as NAAT, to strengthen diagnostic infrastructure and enhance accessibility across diverse settings.</p><p>What to watch: Continued implementation of advanced diagnostic and care initiatives.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thoroughly utilizes all figures, dates, and campaign details from the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/industry/india-records-38-decline-in-tb-mortality-treatment-success-rate-rises-to-94-who/135786683" rel="noopener">ET Healthworld</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
