     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Late Triassic Dinosaur Species Nyana rooiwalensis Identified in South Africa</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Scientists have identified a new dinosaur species, Nyana rooiwalensis, from fossils discovered in South Africa. Dating back over 200 million years, the creature was a sauropodomorph that walked on two legs and weighed approximately 400 kilograms as a youngster.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/new-late-triassic-dinosaur-species-nyana-rooiwalensis-identi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers from the University of the Witwatersrand utilized 3D modeling and bone histology to analyze the specimen. Their findings suggest the dinosaur was in its third year of rapid growth and could have eventually reached multi-tonne body mass. The discovery provides critical data on early sauropodomorph evolution and suggests a potential biogeographical link between southern Africa and North America.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief concisely captures the key scientific findings, the dinosaur's physical characteristics, and the broader evolutionary context provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-newly-south-african-dinosaur-heavyweight.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
