     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Norway Court Invalidates Oil Permits Amidst Cop31 Climate Talks</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cop31</span> <span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#Norway</span> <span>#Fossil Fuels</span></div>
       <p>UN Secretary-General António Guterres delivered a final impassioned speech at Cop31, warning that &quot;You cannot build peace on a burning planet,&quot; and highlighting the vast disparity between scientific demands and political action. Guterres, an outspoken climate activist, celebrated progress over the last decade but underscored that poverty cannot be eradicated amidst climate disasters, and justice is compromised when the most vulnerable suffer disproportionately. He advocated for clean electrification and praised Cop30's roadmaps on transitioning from fossil fuels and addressing deforestation, while also calling for a confrontation of the fossil fuel lobby's influence, stating the planet's future cannot be negotiated on the terms of those profiting from its destruction.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/norway-court-invalidates-oil-permits-amidst-cop31-climate-ta.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Away from the conference, Norway's supreme court made a landmark ruling, declaring the state's decision to award three permits for oil and gas fields in the North Sea invalid. The court found that the government had not adequately accounted for the harm caused by burning these fuels. This decision, hailed by climate campaigners as a significant victory, does not automatically revoke the permits or mandate an immediate shutdown of the fields. Instead, it compels the Norwegian government to consider the global environmental impacts of pollution before granting future licenses. The ruling emerged just two days after Norwegian Prime Minister Jonas Gahr Stør addressed Cop31 leaders, asserting that &quot;Norway will do its part,&quot; a speech notably devoid of any mention of the country's ongoing expansion of oil and gas infrastructure.</p><p>Tessa Khan, executive director of Uplift, described the ruling as a &quot;historical ruling&quot; that would resonate with decision-makers globally. She emphasized that the decision affirms the scientific consensus that &quot;burning more oil and gas is incompatible with a healthy and secure planet.&quot; Khan further stated that, particularly with El Niño looming, the ruling serves as a timely reminder to governments that protecting the public from climate harms is a legal requirement, not a political discussion. Norway has previously argued that its oil and gas production is cleaner than many other major exporters, suggesting that limits on its output would increase European reliance on countries with dirtier fuels and worse human rights records. Studies estimate that for every 10 barrels Norway ceases to produce, other suppliers would generate an additional six to nine barrels. Climate campaigners have dismissed this rationale as &quot;the drug dealer’s defence,&quot; noting that most fossil fuel exporters employ similar justifications to prolong extraction.</p><p>In American politics, US Senator Sheldon Whitehouse is the sole US senator present at Cop31, unofficially representing the country alongside other Democrats. This presence contrasts with former President Donald Trump's consistent absence from Cop delegations for the second consecutive year, during which he has voiced skepticism about climate solutions like solar panels and wind turbines. Senator Whitehouse is pushing for new legislation aimed at accelerating energy project approvals, a contentious proposal currently under fierce debate in the US regarding its environmental implications. Whitehouse outlined his three primary objectives at Cop31: advocating for a price on carbon pollution to dismantle the &quot;free to pollute business model,&quot; highlighting the economic devastation caused by the climate crisis, including a potential &quot;full meltdown&quot; of real estate and insurance markets, and explicitly condemning the fossil fuel industry. He asserted, &quot;If we’re going to win this fight, we’ve got to go at the villains in the fight,&quot; specifically targeting the &quot;climate denial fraud operation&quot; and &quot;dark money corruption operation&quot; of the fossil fuel industry, which he claims have operated semi-covertly for too long. Whitehouse underscored that the crisis &quot;wasn't something that happened. This was something that was done,&quot; equating the omission of this context to telling the story of Little Red Riding Hood without the wolf.</p><p>What to watch: The Norwegian government's response to the court ruling and the ongoing debates at Cop31 regarding fossil fuel transition and climate finance.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article successfully synthesizes the UN summit developments, the Norway court ruling, and the US political context provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/live/2026/nov/13/cop31-climate-crisis-talks-turkey-latest-news-updates" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
