     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Nvidia Faces Environmental Scrutiny Over AI Chip Production</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Under CEO Jensen Huang, Nvidia has become a central driver of the AI boom while facing significant criticism as an environmental liability. The company's chip production has spurred a demand for water- and chemical-intensive factories and significantly strained power grids in data centers.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/nvidia-faces-environmental-scrutiny-over-ai-chip-production.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Nvidia has further clashed with environmental groups regarding &quot;hidden&quot; supply chain emissions, which have ballooned despite Huang's public emphasis on using AI to combat climate change. The company also faces challenges concerning a looming e-waste problem resulting from its rapid technological expansion.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise summary of the environmental concerns raised in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1033051/nvidia-jensen-huang-chips-gpu-supply-chain-climate-pollution" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
