     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Regulator Warns of Action on 137 Anti-Cancer Drug Prices</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#NPPA</span> <span>#Drug Prices</span> <span>#Anti-Cancer Drugs</span> <span>#Healthcare</span></div>
       <p>NEW DELHI: The National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA) has issued a warning of penal action against entities failing to comply with the revised prices of 137 non-scheduled anti-cancer drugs. The authority, which functions under the Department of Pharmaceuticals, has specifically directed State drug controllers (SDCs) to strictly monitor these prices. SDCs are mandated to submit their action taken reports by November 16, according to an official release published on Friday, November 13, 2026.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/regulator-warns-of-action-on-137-anti-cancer-drug-prices.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The revised prices for the 137 anti-cancer drugs came into effect on November 9, 2026. This followed a gazette order issued on October 17, 2026, which capped the trade margin on these specific medicines at 30%. Based on submissions received from pharmaceutical companies up to November 11, the latest revised price lists have been uploaded to the NPPA website, as well as the Pharma Sahi Daam app and portal, the Chemicals and Fertilizers Ministry stated.</p><p>All manufacturers, stockists, dealers, retailers, hospitals, and medical institutions have been explicitly directed to comply with the revised prices. Furthermore, they must prominently display these updated price lists at their premises and on their respective websites. SDCs have been instructed to monitor compliance rigorously across the entire supply chain and within healthcare facilities to ensure that the benefits of the revised maximum retail prices (MRPs) are effectively passed on to patients.</p><p>Any violation of this directive may attract overcharging proceedings under the provisions of the Drug Price Control Order (DPCO), 2013, the release noted. The NPPA has established multiple channels for reporting complaints concerning overcharging, shortage, or non-availability of medicines. These include the Pharma Sahi Daam app or portal, the NPPA helpline (1800-111-255), or via email at monitoring-nppa@gov.in.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report accurately synthesizes all provided details, including specific deadlines, dates, and reporting channels.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/health/comply-with-new-prices-of-137-anti-cancer-drugs-or-face-action-says-regulator/article71698592.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-11-13/regulator-warns-of-action-on-137-anti-cancer-drug-prices" data-title="Regulator Warns of Action on 137 Anti-Cancer Drug Prices"><b>Share:</b>
