     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Researchers Use Chromium to Stabilize Ruthenium in Water Electrolysis</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers at ICFO, in collaboration with international partners, have identified chromium as an effective stabilizer for ruthenium in water electrolysis. While ruthenium is a highly effective single-atom catalyst for producing green hydrogen, it often over-oxidizes and dissolves during operation. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/researchers-use-chromium-to-stabilize-ruthenium-in-water-ele.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Published in Nature Materials, the study shows that neighboring chromium and ruthenium atoms interact through oxygen bridges when placed in an oxide scaffold. This coupling buffers ruthenium’s oxidation and improves catalyst durability by forming cooperative local units. The discovery, facilitated by advanced electron microscopy and synchrotron spectroscopy, provides a new mechanism to maintain catalytic activity during the water-splitting process.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the study published in Nature Materials and the collaborative research effort.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-couple-chromium-ruthenium-grounded-electrolysis.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
