     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Saud Shakeel Joins Exclusive Test Club with Double Nineties at Rawalpindi</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#cricket</span> <span>#Test cricket</span> <span>#Saud Shakeel</span> <span>#Pakistan</span></div>
       <p>Pakistan's middle-order batter Saud Shakeel recorded a rare feat in Test cricket during the first Test against Sri Lanka at Rawalpindi, which concluded in a draw on November 13, 2026. The left-hander scored 93 in the first innings before falling agonizingly short in the second, dismissed for 97 by Lahiru Kumara, just three runs shy of a century. This performance makes him only the fifth individual batter in Test history to be dismissed in the nineties in both innings of the same match.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/saud-shakeel-joins-exclusive-test-club-with-double-nineties-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Shakeel's combined tally of 190 runs across the two innings without converting either into a century places him second on the all-time list for the highest aggregate by a batter dismissed in the nineties on both occasions in the same Test. He trails only Australia's Clem Hill, who amassed 195 runs with scores of 98 and 97 at Adelaide in January 1902. This unusual batting performance has occurred only six times in nearly 150 years of Test cricket. Other notable instances include England's Frank Woolley with 95 and 93 against Australia at Lord's in June 1921, former West Indies opener Gordon Greenidge scoring 91 and 97 against New Zealand at Christchurch in February 1980, and Sri Lanka's Mahela Jayawardene registering 92 and 96 against New Zealand at Colombo in August 2009.</p><p>In the Rawalpindi Test, Pakistan batted first and declared their opening innings at 434 for 9. Sri Lanka responded with 446 runs, securing a narrow first-innings lead. Pakistan then declared their second innings at 284 for 8, setting Sri Lanka a target of 272 runs. However, the match ran out of time and ultimately ended in a draw. Shakeel's twin efforts in the nineties stood out as the primary individual contribution of the match. The two-match series is set to continue at the same venue, with the second Test scheduled to commence on November 17, 2026.</p><p>What to watch: The second Test at Rawalpindi, starting November 17, 2026, will determine the series outcome.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft captures all key details, historical context, and statistical significance of the performance accurately from the provided source.</p>
