     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Ars Technica · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Slate Auto Introduces Screenless Navigation for Electric Truck</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Startup Slate Auto is launching an affordable electric truck priced under $25,000, which notably lacks an embedded infotainment system. The vehicle features a basic dashboard display for speed and range, but excludes connectivity to encourage users to utilize their own devices.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/slate-auto-introduces-screenless-navigation-for-electric-tru.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>To address navigation needs, the company is providing a spiral-bound road atlas as a &quot;Screenless GPS&quot; solution. Slate Auto claims this approach aligns with the preferences of past generations and prioritizes simplicity, allowing owners to customize their experience without reliance on integrated digital technology.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately captures the unconventional navigation approach, though it slightly conflates the company's marketing tone with factual claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/11/screenless-gps-slate-auto-says-remember-the-road-atlas/" rel="noopener">Ars Technica</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
