     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · BBC Science · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>SpaceX Attempts Historic Double Catch of Starship Rocket</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#SpaceX</span> <span>#Starship</span> <span>#Rocketry</span> <span>#Nasa</span></div>
       <p>SpaceX is preparing for its next test flight on Friday, aiming to achieve a significant milestone: the simultaneous mid-air recovery of both parts of its massive Starship rocket. Elon Musk's company plans to use the mechanical arms, affectionately nicknamed &quot;chopsticks,&quot; on its launch tower to grab the rocket's sections as they descend back to Earth. If successful, this endeavor would mark the first full recovery of a rocket that has reached space, critically advancing SpaceX's ambition to make Starship fully reusable for repeated flights.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/spacex-attempts-historic-double-catch-of-starship-rocket.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The 124m-tall rocket is scheduled for liftoff from the Starbase launchpad in Texas at 1645 local time (2245 GMT), within a 75-minute launch window. The mission's first recovery attempt will occur approximately six and a half minutes after launch, as the lower section, the Super Heavy booster, returns to the launch tower. Following this, the upper part of the rocket, known as the Ship, will continue its journey into space, deploying 26 satellites to expand the Starlink constellation, which provides high-speed internet. The Ship is then projected to complete 14 orbits around Earth before making its second catch attempt about 22 and a half hours after takeoff.</p><p>Previously, SpaceX has successfully caught the Super Heavy Booster on three separate test flights. However, the upper stage of the rocket, which only achieved orbit for the first time in September, has never been recovered by the chopsticks, instead splashing down in the ocean. SpaceX has stated that the decision to attempt catching the Ship will depend on a careful assessment of the vehicle's performance while in space, with various points along the flight path allowing for an ocean splashdown if problems are detected.</p><p>Building a fully reusable rocket has been a long-standing goal for SpaceX. The company made history in 2015 by becoming the first to vertically land part of a rocket with its Falcon 9 booster. Today, Falcon 9 rockets fly over a hundred times annually, and the reusability of their bottom sections has dramatically reduced launch costs. Prof Malcolm Macdonald, President of the Royal Aeronautical Society, notes that while a successful double catch would be &quot;visually spectacular,&quot; full reusability holds the potential to &quot;fundamentally change the economics of accessing space.&quot; He added that one flight would not prove the model but could signify an &quot;important transition&quot; from demonstrating Starship's flight capability to its operational viability.</p><p>Both SpaceX shareholders, following the company's recent public offering, and the US Space Agency will be closely monitoring this flight. Starship's progress is directly relevant to Nasa's ambitious timeline of landing humans on the Moon by 2028 as part of its Artemis program. SpaceX holds a Nasa contract to configure the Ship into a lander that will eventually transport astronauts to the lunar surface. However, SpaceX is not the sole partner; Blue Origin, owned by Jeff Bezos, also has a contract for its New Glenn rocket and lander for Moon landings.</p><p>Both companies are slated to play crucial roles in Artemis III, currently planned for summer 2027, another challenging schedule. This mission envisions four astronauts aboard Nasa's Orion spacecraft attempting to dock with both SpaceX and Blue Origin's landers. Meeting these commitments requires significant progress from Starship in the coming months, as its development pace has been slower than SpaceX's initial intentions. Blue Origin also faces challenges, having suffered a major setback this year when its rocket exploded, destroying its Florida launchpad and delaying its return to flight to early 2027 from the end of this year. Nasa Administrator Jared Isaacman has underscored the urgency, stating America is in a race with China, which aims to land its Taikonauts on the lunar surface by 2030.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the upcoming test flight, the technological context, and the geopolitical implications noted in the text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckregdgnl0xpo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Science</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
