     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Startup Drogue Secures $20 Million to Develop Ocean-Powered Data Centers</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Washington, D.C.-based startup Drogue has raised $20 million to develop floating data centers powered by ocean thermal energy conversion. The company, founded six months ago, aims to address the massive surge in AI energy demand by bypassing traditional utility-constrained real estate timelines.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/startup-drogue-secures-20-million-to-develop-ocean-powered-d.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Drogue plans to launch a multi-megawatt prototype on an oceangoing ship by early 2028. The technology functions like a reverse heat pump, utilizing the temperature differential between warm surface water and cold deep water to generate electricity. This approach aims to leverage the ocean's vast energy reserves to support data center operations on floating platforms.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the startup's funding, technological approach, and timeline while remaining well within the word count target.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/13/this-young-startup-wants-to-power-floating-data-centers-with-the-oceans-heat-and-investors-are-already-on-board/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
