     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Supreme Court Upholds Second Marriage Inheritance Rights for Delhi Land</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#personal finance</span> <span>#inheritance</span> <span>#property rights</span> <span>#Hindu Succession Act</span></div>
       <p>The Supreme Court recently clarified inheritance rights concerning ancestral property, ruling that a woman’s interest in land could become absolute ownership, allowing children from a second marriage to inherit. This decision, in the case of Sultan Singh (Dead) through LRs and Others vs Financial Commissioner, Government of NCT of Delhi and Others, was handed down on October 9, 2026. The Court upheld the inheritance claim of two sons, Chand Ram and Chander Bhan, born to a woman from her second marriage, over 45 bighas of agricultural land in Bhawana village, Delhi, rejecting claims from her first husband’s family.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/supreme-court-upholds-second-marriage-inheritance-rights-for.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The dispute originated from Mauji Ram, who married Khajano and had a son, Ram Chander. After Mauji Ram died, Ram Chander also passed away in 1937, leaving Khajano as the sole surviving representative of their estate. Khajano subsequently remarried Bal Kishan, with whom she had Chand Ram and Chander Bhan. Khajano was recognized as a Bhumidhar when the Delhi Land Reforms Act came into force on July 20, 1954. She died in 1973, and it was in 1997 that her two sons from the second marriage applied to have the 45 bighas of land mutated in their names in revenue records. This application was challenged by male descendants of Mauji Ram’s brothers, who contended that Khajano had only inherited a limited, life interest in the land, meaning it should pass to the nearest surviving heirs of the last male proprietor under Section 50 of the Delhi Land Reforms Act upon her death.</p><p>The central legal question was whether Khajano still held a limited estate upon her death in 1973, or if her rights had transformed into absolute ownership under the Hindu Succession Act. The Court acknowledged that under the Hindu Women’s Rights to Property Act, 1937, a widow inherited only a limited interest, which was Khajano’s original position. However, Section 14(1) of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, stipulates that any property possessed by a Hindu woman, whether acquired before or after the law’s enactment, is to be held by her as a full owner, not a limited one. This section’s explanation explicitly includes property acquired through inheritance. The Supreme Court concluded that Section 14(1) effectively enlarged Khajano’s interest into absolute ownership from June 17, 1956, meaning she was an absolute owner when succession opened in 1973. The court also clarified that Section 14(1) converts an existing limited interest into full ownership when statutory conditions are met, rather than creating a new property right where none existed.</p><p>The appellants argued that personal law should be frozen as of 1954, but the Supreme Court disagreed, stating that the relevant personal law is that applicable when the woman dies and succession opens. Since Khajano died after the Hindu Succession Act came into force and her limited interest had become absolute, succession was governed by Section 51(2)(a)(ii), read with Section 53, of the Delhi Land Reforms Act. The appeal was dismissed, leaving the mutation in favor of her two sons undisturbed. For ordinary savers and taxpayers, this ruling reinforces the principle that a Hindu woman's inherited property rights can evolve into absolute ownership, which then dictates who inherits upon her demise. However, the decision specifically pertains to agricultural land in Delhi under the Delhi Land Reforms Act. It does not imply that children from a second marriage automatically inherit all property associated with a woman’s first marriage, nor that all ancestral property disputes will follow this exact precedent. Each case will require careful examination of the property’s source of title, the woman’s original interest, her continued possession, and the specific succession laws applicable.</p><p>What to watch: The specific circumstances of inherited property and applicable laws will continue to be crucial in future cases.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the provided source text, covering the legal arguments, the specific case details, and the nuances of the court's ruling.</p>
