     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-13 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tata Motors PV Narrows Q2FY27 Loss to ₹5,451 Crore Amidst 34% Revenue Growth</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Business</span> <span>#Automotive</span> <span>#Financial Results</span> <span>#Tata Motors</span></div>
       <p>Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles (TMPV) reported a consolidated net loss of ₹5,451 crore from continuing operations for the second quarter of financial year 2026-27 (Q2FY27), narrowing from ₹6,368 crore a year earlier. Consolidated revenue from operations surged 34 percent year-on-year to ₹96,920 crore, up from ₹72,349 crore. This improvement was largely driven by recovering Jaguar Land Rover (JLR) volumes, which helped offset significant exceptional charges of ₹6,738 crore incurred during the quarter, primarily for employee separation costs and asset impairments.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/tata-motors-pv-narrows-q2fy27-loss-to-5451-crore-amidst-34-r.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>JLR's revenue rose 20.3 percent year-on-year to £5.89 billion, with wholesale volumes recovering in the UK and overseas markets. Despite this, lower volumes in China and West Asia, alongside an adverse product mix, continued to impact profitability. JLR's profit before tax (PBT) and exceptional items stood at £6 million, a notable recovery from a loss of £485 million a year earlier. The British luxury-car maker recognized £530 million in exceptional charges, with £295 million allocated to a voluntary redundancy program targeting around 4,000 employees. Amid ongoing geopolitical uncertainties and inflationary pressures, JLR revised its full-year guidance to an adjusted Ebit margin of 2-3 percent and a free cash outflow of approximately £1.25 billion.</p><p>Domestically, TMPV's passenger vehicle business achieved its highest-ever quarterly revenue of ₹20,423 crore, marking a 50.9 percent year-on-year growth. However, its Ebitda margin contracted by 180 basis points to 4 percent. Shailesh Chandra, MD &amp; CEO of TMPV, noted that only about 40 percent of rising commodity costs were offset through calibrated price increases and cost reductions, with the remaining pressure impacting profitability. The company expects steel, rubber, and copper prices to remain elevated in Q3FY27 and is considering a larger price increase in Q4FY27 to improve margins, which are projected to remain around current levels in Q3.</p><p>TMPV plans to ramp up electric vehicle (EV) supplies to nearly 20,000 units a month over the next two to three months, up from approximately 16,000 currently, as demand continues to outstrip supply. Chandra stated that the company currently meets only about 60-65 percent of EV demand, with monthly supplies having already increased from 9,000 units in March-April to 16,000 units. Factories are operating at full capacity, and the company is closely monitoring 26 components to support this production ramp-up. India's PV industry is expected to grow around 12-13 percent in FY27, moderating to about 5 percent in H2FY27.</p><p>What to watch: Tata Motors' Q4FY27 pricing strategies and the impact of increased EV production.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft covers the key financial figures, operational challenges, and management outlook provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/companies/quarterly-results/tata-motors-pv-q2-net-loss-at-5-531-cr-revenue-misses-estimates-126111301014_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
