     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tax Risks Associated with Benami Property Purchases in India</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#finance</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Purchasing property in the name of a spouse, child, or parent using personal funds can trigger income tax scrutiny or action under the Benami Transactions (Prohibition) Act. Authorities may investigate the source of funds if the registered owner lacks independent income to justify the investment.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-13/tax-risks-associated-with-benami-property-purchases-in-india.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>To avoid penalties or property confiscation, buyers must clearly document financial arrangements. Gifts to specified relatives are generally tax-exempt under Section 56(2)(x) of the Income-tax Act, while loans require formal written agreements with traceable repayment through banking channels. A family relationship does not grant immunity from legal requirements or tax demands.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides a clear and accurate summary of tax risks and legal requirements regarding property purchases in relatives' names.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/buying-property-in-your-wife-s-or-parents-name-know-income-tax-rules-benami-property-risks-and-penalties-in-india-11794580967194.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
